>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (Student academic performance is shaped by a wide range of behavioural, familial, and institutional factors, yet many of these influences are rarely examined together in a single quantitative framework. This project analyses the Student Performance Factors dataset, comprising 6,607 student records and 20 variables covering study habits, attendance, parental involvement, access to resources, and related factors, with the goal of understanding and predicting student Exam Score. The dataset was cleaned by removing duplicate records, imputing missing categorical values with the column mode, and correcting an implausible outlier value in the target variable. Exploratory and statistical analysis showed that Attendance \(r = 0.581\) and Hours Studied \(r = 0.446\) were the variables most strongly associated with Exam Score, while sleep hours and physical activity showed negligible linear association. Categorical variables such as Parental Involvement and Access to Resources were ordinally encoded, nominal variables were one-hot encoded, and an Engagement Index and Study-to-Sleep Ratio were engineered as additional predictors. A Linear Regression model and a Random Forest Regressor were trained on an 80/20 train-test split. The Linear Regression model achieved the stronger generalisation performance, with a test R-squared of 0.7701 and a Root Mean Squared Error of 1.803 points, compared with an R- squared of 0.65 for the Random Forest model. These results indicate that a large share of the variation in exam performance can be explained by a compact set of behavioural and contextual factors, with attendance and study engagement emerging as the strongest levers. The findings suggest practical, low- cost interventions - such as attendance monitoring and structured study-support programs - that schools could adopt to improve student outcomes.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
