197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (Academic performance is one of the most closely watched outcomes in education systems worldwide, influencing student opportunities, institutional rankings, and public policy. While traditional educational research has long recognised that performance is multi-causal, most classroom-level decisions are still made on intuition rather than data. The growing availability of structured student-level datasets, capturing study habits, attendance, family background, and school characteristics, creates an opportunity to apply data science techniques to identify which factors most strongly influence outcomes, and to what extent those outcomes can be predicted in advance.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 147 0 R
/T (1.2 Context of the Problem)
/E (1.2 Context of the Problem)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (Teachers, school administrators, and parents are the primary stakeholders who could benefit from a clearer, quantitative understanding of what drives exam performance. In practice, resources for interventions such as tutoring, counselling, or attendance monitoring are limited, so knowing which factors carry the most predictive weight helps target these resources more effectively. A predictive model built on historical student data can also serve as an early-warning tool, flagging students whose combination of study habits and circumstances puts them at risk of underperforming.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 147 0 R
/T (1.3 Problem Statement and Motivation)
/E (1.3 Problem Statement and Motivation)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (Given a set of behavioural, familial, and institutional factors describing a student, can we accurately estimate that student's final exam score, and which of these factors contribute most to the outcome? Educators often lack a systematic, data-driven way to identify which students are likely to underperform and why. A regression-based approach that both predicts the outcome and quantifies the relative importance of each input factor can provide actionable, evidence-based guidance for designing targeted academic support programs, rather than relying on generic, one-size-fits-all interventions.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 147 0 R
/T (1.4 Scope of the Study)
/E (1.4 Scope of the Study)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (This study uses a single cross-sectional dataset of student records containing study, attendance, and demographic/contextual variables, along with a final Exam Score. The project covers data cleaning, exploratory data analysis, statistical analysis, feature engineering, and the development and evaluation of regression models to predict Exam Score. It does not cover longitudinal tracking of the same students over time, does not incorporate psychometric or classroom-observation data, and does not attempt to establish causal relationships between the study variables and performance; all relationships reported are associative, not causal. The primary objectives and prediction question that follow \(Section 2\) define the boundaries of the analysis in more detail.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
