428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (The figures below were selected because each answers a specific question raised during EDA rather than serving as a general chart dump.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (Attendance and Hours Studied are clearly the two numeric variables most strongly associated with Exam Score, followed by Previous Scores and Tutoring Sessions. Sleep Hours and Physical Activity show correlations very close to zero, suggesting limited direct linear association with performance within the range observed in this dataset. For the key categorical variables, mean Exam Score was compared across group levels: mean scores rise consistently from Low to Medium to High Parental Involvement and from Negative to Neutral to Positive Peer Influence \(see the boxplots in Section 7, Figures 2 and 3\), indicating that both family involvement and social environment are associated with better outcomes, alongside the individual behavioural factors.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 152 0 R
/T (6.3 What the EDA Tells Us About Modelling Choices)
/E (6.3 What the EDA Tells Us About Modelling Choices)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (These exploratory findings directly shaped the modelling approach: Attendance, Hours Studied, Previous Scores and Tutoring Sessions were retained as core numeric predictors; the ordered categorical variables \(Parental Involvement, Access to Resources, etc.\) were preserved as ordinal features rather than being dropped, since their group-level averages show a clear monotonic trend; and the correlation matrix \(Figure 6, Section 7\) confirmed no pair of numeric predictors was correlated strongly enough \(> 0.7\) to raise serious multicollinearity concerns for the Linear Regression model.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
