1282 0 obj
<<
/Title (Most behavioural variables \(Hours Studied, Attendance, Sleep Hours, Previous Scores\) are approximately symmetric \(skewness close to 0\), so their mean and median are very close and either is a representative measure of centre. Tutoring Sessions is more right-skewed \(skew = 0.82\), reflecting that most students attend few tutoring sessions while a minority attend many; for this variable the median \(1 session\) is a more representative 'typical' value than the mean, which is pulled upward by the small number of high- tutoring students. Exam Score is mildly right-skewed after cleaning \(skew = 1.64\), again meaning the median is a slightly more robust summary of typical performance than the mean, although the two remain close in practice.)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1272 0 R
/Next 1283 0 R
>>
endobj
1283 0 obj
<<
/Title (It is important to note that the correlations reported in Section 6.2 \(e.g. Attendance and Hours Studied with Exam Score\) describe association, not causation. Students who attend more and study more may also differ in other unmeasured ways \(such as motivation or family support\) that independently affect performance; the regression model in Sections 9-10 quantifies predictive association, not a causal effect of any single variable.)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1272 0 R
/Prev 1282 0 R
>>
endobj
1284 0 obj
<<
/Title (This is a supervised regression problem: the goal is to predict a continuous target variable, Exam Score, from a set of numeric and encoded categorical input features \(Section 5.5\). The final feature set was used as input \(X\), and Exam_Score as the target \(y\). No feature directly derived from or revealing the exam outcome was included, so no target leakage is present.)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1273 0 R
>>
endobj
